## sn38.3
sn38.3:1.1 | S: “Reverend Sāriputta, who in the world have principled speech? Who in the world practice well? Who are the holy ones in the world?” | T: On one occasion Ven. Sāriputta was staying in Magadha near Nāla Village. Then Jambukhādaka the wanderer went to Ven. Sāriputta and, on arrival, exchanged courteous greetings with him. After an exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, he sat to one side. As he was sitting there he said to Ven. Sāriputta: “Friend Sāriputta, who in the world are speakers of Dhamma? Who in the world are those who practice rightly? Who in the world are well-gone?”
sn38.3:1.2 | S: “Reverend, those who teach principles for giving up greed, hate, and delusion have principled speech in the world. | T: “Friend, those who teach the Dhamma for the abandoning of passion, for the abandoning of aversion, for the abandoning of delusion: They are speakers of Dhamma in the world.
sn38.3:1.3 | S: Those who practice for giving up greed, hate, and delusion are practicing well in the world. | T: Those who practice the Dhamma for the abandoning of passion, for the abandoning of aversion, for the abandoning of delusion: They are those who practice rightly in the world.
sn38.3:1.4 | S: Those who have given up greed, hate, and delusion—so they’re cut off at the root, made like a palm stump, obliterated, and unable to arise in the future—are holy ones in the world.” | T: Those whose passion has been abandoned—its root destroyed, made like a palmyra stump, deprived of the conditions of development, not destined for future arising; whose aversion has been abandoned… whose delusion has been abandoned—its root destroyed, made like a palmyra stump, deprived of the conditions of development, not destined for future arising: They in the world are well-gone.”
sn38.3:2.1 | S: “But, reverend, is there a path and a practice for giving up that greed, hate, and delusion?” | T: “But is there a path, is there a practice for the abandoning of this passion, aversion, & delusion?”
sn38.3:2.2 | S: “There is, reverend.” | T: “Yes, there is a path, there is a practice for the abandoning of this passion, aversion, & delusion.”
sn38.3:2.3 | S: “Well, what is it?” | T: “Then what is the path, what is the practice for the abandoning of this passion, aversion, & delusion?”
sn38.3:2.4 | S: “It is simply this noble eightfold path, that is: | T: “Precisely this noble eightfold path, my friend:
sn38.3:2.5 | S: right view, right purpose, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, and right immersion. | T: right view, right resolve, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, right concentration.
sn38.3:2.6 | S: This is the path, this is the practice for giving up that greed, hate, and delusion.” | T: This is the path, this is the practice for the abandoning of this passion, aversion, & delusion.”
sn38.3:2.7 | S: “This is a fine path, a fine practice, for giving up greed, hate, and delusion. | T: “It’s an auspicious path, my friend, an auspicious practice for the abandoning of this passion, aversion, & delusion—
sn38.3:2.8 | S: Just this much is enough for diligence.” | T: enough for the sake of heedfulness.”
sn38.3:2.9 | P: Tatiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn38.4
sn38.4:1.1 | S: “Reverend Sāriputta, what’s the goal of leading the spiritual life under the ascetic Gotama?” | T: On one occasion Ven. Sāriputta was staying in Magadha near Nāla Village. Then Jambukhādaka the wanderer went to Ven. Sāriputta and, on arrival, exchanged courteous greetings with him. After an exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, he sat to one side. As he was sitting there he said to Ven. Sāriputta: “Friend Sāriputta, with what purpose is the holy life lived under Gotama the contemplative?”
sn38.4:1.2 | S: “The goal of leading the spiritual life under the Buddha is to completely understand suffering.” | T: “The holy life is lived under the Blessed One with the purpose of the full comprehension of stress & suffering.”
sn38.4:1.3 | S: “But, reverend, is there a path and a practice for completely understanding this suffering?” | T: “But is there a path, is there a practice for the full comprehension of this stress & suffering?”
sn38.4:1.4 | S: “There is.” … | T: “Yes, there is a path, there is a practice for the full comprehension of this stress & suffering.”
sn38.4:1.5 | P: “Katamo panāvuso, maggo katamā paṭipadā, etassa dukkhassa pariññāyā”ti? | T: “Then what is the path, what is the practice for the full comprehension of this stress & suffering?”
sn38.4:2.1 | P: “Ayameva kho, āvuso, ariyo aṭṭhaṅgiko maggo, etassa dukkhassa pariññāya, seyyathidaṁ— | T: “Precisely this noble eightfold path, my friend:
sn38.4:2.2 | P: sammādiṭṭhi …pe… sammāsamādhi. | T: right view, right resolve, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, right concentration.
sn38.4:2.3 | P: Ayaṁ kho, āvuso, maggo ayaṁ paṭipadā etassa dukkhassa pariññāyā”ti. | T: This is the path, this is the practice for the full comprehension of this stress & suffering.”
sn38.4:2.4 | P: “Bhaddako, āvuso, maggo bhaddikā paṭipadā, etassa dukkhassa pariññāya. | T: “It’s an auspicious path, my friend, an auspicious practice for the full comprehension of this stress & suffering—
sn38.4:2.5 | P: Alañca panāvuso sāriputta, appamādāyā”ti. | T: enough for the sake of heedfulness.”
sn38.4:2.6 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn38.5
sn38.5:1.1 | S: “Reverend Sāriputta, they speak of this thing called ‘gaining solace’. | T: On one occasion Ven. Sāriputta was staying in Magadha near Nāla Village. Then Jambukhādaka the wanderer went to Ven. Sāriputta and, on arrival, exchanged courteous greetings with him. After an exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, he sat to one side. As he was sitting there he said to Ven. Sāriputta: “‘A person who has attained assurance, a person who has attained assurance,’ it is said, my friend Sāriputta.
sn38.5:1.2 | S: At what point do you gain solace?” | T: To what extent is one a person who has attained assurance?”
sn38.5:1.3 | S: “When a mendicant truly understands the six fields of contact’s origin, disappearance, gratification, drawback, and escape, at that point they’ve gained solace.” | T: “Friend, when a monk discerns, as it has come to be, the origination, passing away, allure, drawbacks of—and escape from—the six media of (sensory) contact: It’s to this extent that he is a person who has attained assurance.”
sn38.5:1.4 | S: “But, reverend, is there a path and a practice for realizing this solace?” | T: “But is there a path, is there a practice for the realization of this assurance?”
sn38.5:1.5 | S: “There is.” … | T: “Yes, there is a path, there is a practice for the realization of this assurance.”
sn38.5:1.6 | P: “Katamo panāvuso, maggo katamā paṭipadā, etassa assāsassa sacchikiriyāyā”ti? | T: “Then what is the path, what is the practice for the realization of this assurance?”
sn38.5:1.7 | P: “Ayameva kho, āvuso, ariyo aṭṭhaṅgiko maggo etassa assāsassa sacchikiriyāya, seyyathidaṁ— | T: “Precisely this noble eightfold path, my friend:
sn38.5:1.8 | P: sammādiṭṭhi …pe… sammāsamādhi. | T: right view, right resolve, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, right concentration.
sn38.5:1.9 | P: Ayaṁ kho, āvuso, maggo ayaṁ paṭipadā, etassa assāsassa sacchikiriyāyā”ti. | T: This is the path, this is the practice for the realization of this assurance.”
sn38.5:1.10 | P: “Bhaddako, āvuso, maggo bhaddikā paṭipadā, etassa assāsassa sacchikiriyāya. | T: “It’s an auspicious path, my friend, an auspicious practice for realization of this assurance—
sn38.5:1.11 | P: Alañca panāvuso sāriputta, appamādāyā”ti. | T: enough for the sake of heedfulness.”
sn38.5:1.12 | P: Pañcamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn38.6
sn38.6:1.1 | S: “Reverend Sāriputta, they speak of this thing called ‘gaining ultimate solace’. | T: On one occasion Ven. Sāriputta was staying in Magadha near Nāla Village. Then Jambukhādaka the wanderer went to Ven. Sāriputta and, on arrival, exchanged courteous greetings with him. After an exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, he sat to one side. As he was sitting there he said to Ven. Sāriputta: “‘A person who has attained foremost assurance, a person who has attained foremost assurance,’ it is said, my friend Sāriputta.
sn38.6:1.2 | S: At what point do you gain ultimate solace?” | T: To what extent is one a person who has attained foremost assurance?”
sn38.6:1.3 | S: “When a mendicant is freed by not grasping after truly understanding the six fields of contact’s origin, disappearance, gratification, drawback, and escape, at that point they’ve gained ultimate solace.” | T: “Friend, when a monk—having known, as it has come to be, the origination, passing away, allure, drawbacks of, and escape from, the six media of (sensory) contact—is released through no clinging: It’s to this extent that he is a person who has attained foremost assurance.”
sn38.6:1.4 | S: “But, reverend, is there a path and a practice for realizing this ultimate solace?” | T: “But is there a path, is there a practice for the realization of this foremost assurance?”
sn38.6:1.5 | S: “There is.” … | T: “Yes, there is a path, there is a practice for the realization of this foremost assurance.”
sn38.6:1.6 | P: “Katamo pana, āvuso, maggo katamā paṭipadā, etassa paramassāsassa sacchikiriyāyā”ti? | T: “Then what is the path, what is the practice for the realization of this foremost assurance?”
sn38.6:1.7 | P: “Ayameva kho, āvuso, ariyo aṭṭhaṅgiko maggo etassa paramassāsassa sacchikiriyāya, seyyathidaṁ— | T: “Precisely this noble eightfold path, my friend:
sn38.6:1.8 | P: sammādiṭṭhi …pe… sammāsamādhi. | T: right view, right resolve, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, right concentration.

