## sn38.8
sn38.8:1.1 | S: “Reverend Sāriputta, they speak of this thing called ‘defilement’. | T: On one occasion Ven. Sāriputta was staying in Magadha near Nāla Village. Then Jambukhādaka the wanderer went to Ven. Sāriputta and, on arrival, exchanged courteous greetings with him. After an exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, he sat to one side. As he was sitting there he said to Ven. Sāriputta: “‘Effluent, effluent,’ it is said, my friend Sāriputta.
sn38.8:1.2 | S: What is defilement?” | T: Which effluent (are they referring to)?”
sn38.8:1.3 | S: “Reverend, there are three defilements. | T: “There are these three effluents:
sn38.8:1.4 | S: The defilements of sensuality, desire to be reborn, and ignorance. | T: the effluent of sensuality, the effluent of becoming, the effluent of ignorance.
sn38.8:1.5 | S: These are the three defilements.” | T: These are the three effluents.”
sn38.8:1.6 | S: “But, reverend, is there a path and a practice for completely understanding these three defilements?” | T: “But is there a path, is there a practice for the abandoning of these effluents?”
sn38.8:1.7 | S: “There is.” … | T: “Yes, there is a path, there is a practice for the abandoning of these effluents.”
sn38.8:1.8 | P: “Katamo panāvuso, maggo katamā paṭipadā etesaṁ āsavānaṁ pahānāyā”ti? | T: “Then what is the path, what is the practice for the abandoning of these effluents?”
sn38.8:1.9 | P: “Ayameva kho, āvuso, ariyo aṭṭhaṅgiko maggo etesaṁ āsavānaṁ pahānāya, seyyathidaṁ— | T: “Precisely this noble eightfold path, my friend:
sn38.8:1.10 | P: sammādiṭṭhi …pe… sammāsamādhi. | T: right view, right resolve, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, right concentration.
sn38.8:1.11 | P: Ayaṁ kho, āvuso, maggo ayaṁ paṭipadā, etesaṁ āsavānaṁ pahānāyā”ti. | T: This is the path, this is the practice for the abandoning of these effluents.”
sn38.8:1.12 | P: “Bhaddako, āvuso, maggo bhaddikā paṭipadā, etesaṁ āsavānaṁ pahānāya. | T: “It’s an auspicious path, my friend, an auspicious practice for the abandoning of these effluents—
sn38.8:1.13 | P: Alañca panāvuso sāriputta, appamādāyā”ti. | T: enough for the sake of heedfulness.”
sn38.8:1.14 | P: Aṭṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn38.9
sn38.9:1.1 | S: “Reverend Sāriputta, they speak of this thing called ‘ignorance’. | T: On one occasion Ven. Sāriputta was staying in Magadha near Nāla Village. Then Jambukhādaka the wanderer went to Ven. Sāriputta and, on arrival, exchanged courteous greetings with him. After an exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, he sat to one side. As he was sitting there he said to Ven. Sāriputta: “‘Ignorance, ignorance,’ it is said, my friend Sāriputta.
sn38.9:1.2 | S: What is ignorance?” | T: Which ignorance (are they referring to)?”
sn38.9:1.3 | S: “Not knowing about suffering, the origin of suffering, the cessation of suffering, and the practice that leads to the cessation of suffering. | T: “Not knowing in terms of stress, not knowing in terms of the origination of stress, not knowing in terms of the cessation of stress, not knowing in terms of the path of practice for the cessation of stress:
sn38.9:1.4 | S: This is called ignorance.” | T: This is called ignorance.”
sn38.9:1.5 | S: “But, reverend, is there a path and a practice for giving up that ignorance?” | T: “But is there a path, is there a practice for the abandoning of this ignorance?”
sn38.9:1.6 | S: “There is.” … | T: “Yes, there is a path, there is a practice for the abandoning of this ignorance.”
sn38.9:1.7 | P: “Katamo panāvuso, maggo katamā paṭipadā, etissā avijjāya pahānāyā”ti? | T: “Then what is the path, what is the practice for the abandoning of this ignorance?”
sn38.9:1.8 | P: “Ayameva kho, āvuso, ariyo aṭṭhaṅgiko maggo, etissā avijjāya pahānāya, seyyathidaṁ— | T: “Precisely this noble eightfold path, my friend:
sn38.9:1.9 | P: sammādiṭṭhi …pe… sammāsamādhi. | T: right view, right resolve, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, right concentration.
sn38.9:1.10 | P: Ayaṁ kho, āvuso, maggo ayaṁ paṭipadā, etissā avijjāya pahānāyā”ti. | T: This is the path, this is the practice for the abandoning of this ignorance.”
sn38.9:1.11 | P: “Bhaddako, āvuso, maggo bhaddikā paṭipadā, etissā avijjāya pahānāya. | T: “It’s an auspicious path, my friend, an auspicious practice for the abandoning of these this ignorance—
sn38.9:1.12 | P: Alañca panāvuso sāriputta, appamādāyā”ti. | T: enough for the sake of heedfulness.”
sn38.9:1.13 | P: Navamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn4.19
sn4.19:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: Near Sāvatthī.
sn4.19:1.2 | S: Now at that time the Buddha was educating, encouraging, firing up, and inspiring the mendicants with a Dhamma talk about extinguishment. | T: Now at that time the Blessed One was instructing, urging, rousing, & encouraging the monks with a Dhamma talk concerning unbinding.
sn4.19:1.3 | S: And those mendicants were paying attention, applying the mind, concentrating wholeheartedly, and actively listening. | T: The monks—attentive, interested, lending ear, focusing their entire awareness—were listening to the Dhamma.
sn4.19:2.1 | S: Then Māra thought, | T: Then the thought occurred to Māra, the Evil One:
sn4.19:2.2 | S: “The ascetic Gotama is giving a Dhamma talk about extinguishment … and the mendicants are listening well. | T: “Gotama the contemplative is instructing, urging, rousing, & encouraging the monks with a Dhamma talk concerning unbinding. The monks—attentive, interested, lending ear, focusing their entire awareness—are listening to the Dhamma.
sn4.19:2.3 | S: Why don’t I go and pull the wool over their eyes?” | T: What if I were to go to Gotama the contemplative to obscure his vision?”
sn4.19:2.4 | S: Then Māra the Wicked manifested in the form of a farmer carrying a large plough on his shoulder. He held a long goad, his hair was messy, he was clad in sunn hemp, and his feet were muddy. He went up to the Buddha and said to him, | T: Then Māra the Evil One, taking on the form of a farmer with a large plowshare over his shoulder, carrying a long goad stick—his hair disheveled, his clothes made of coarse hemp, his feet splattered with mud—went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, said,
sn4.19:2.5 | S: “So, ascetic, did you happen to see any oxen?” | T: “Hey, contemplative. Have you seen my oxen?”
sn4.19:2.6 | S: “But what have you to do with oxen, Wicked One?” | T: “And what are your oxen, Evil One?”
sn4.19:2.7 | S: “Mine alone, ascetic, is the eye, mine are sights, mine is the field of eye contact consciousness. | T: “Mine alone is the eye, contemplative. Mine are forms, mine is the dimension of consciousness & contact at the eye.
sn4.19:2.8 | S: Where can you escape me, ascetic? | T: Where can you go to escape me?
sn4.19:2.9 | S: Mine alone is the ear … | T: Mine alone is the ear…
sn4.19:2.10 | S: nose … | T: the nose…
sn4.19:2.11 | S: tongue … | T: the tongue…
sn4.19:2.12 | S: body … | T: the body…
sn4.19:2.13 | S: mind, mine are ideas, mine is the field of mind contact consciousness. | T: Mine alone is the intellect, contemplative. Mine are ideas, mine is the dimension of consciousness & contact at the intellect.
sn4.19:2.14 | S: Where can you escape me, ascetic?” | T: Where can you go to escape me?”
sn4.19:3.1 | S: “Yours alone, Wicked One, is the eye, yours are sights, yours is the field of eye contact consciousness. | T: “Yours alone is the eye, Evil One. Yours are forms, yours is the sphere of consciousness & contact at the eye.
sn4.19:3.2 | S: Where there is no eye, no sights, no eye contact consciousness—you have no place there, Wicked One! | T: Where no eye exists, no forms exist, no dimension of consciousness & contact at the eye exists: There, Evil One, you cannot go.
sn4.19:3.3 | S: Yours alone is the ear … | T: Yours alone is the ear…
sn4.19:3.4 | P: Yattha ca kho, pāpima, natthi sotaṁ, natthi saddā, natthi sotasamphassaviññāṇāyatanaṁ, agati tava tattha, pāpima. | T: (none)
sn4.19:3.5 | S: nose … | T: the nose…
sn4.19:3.6 | P: Yattha ca kho, pāpima, natthi ghānaṁ, natthi gandhā, natthi ghānasamphassaviññāṇāyatanaṁ, agati tava tattha, pāpima. | T: (none)
sn4.19:3.7 | S: tongue … | T: the tongue…
sn4.19:3.8 | S: body … | T: the body…
sn4.19:3.9 | S: mind, yours are ideas, yours is the field of mind contact consciousness. | T: Yours alone is the intellect, Evil One. Yours are ideas, yours is the dimension of consciousness & contact at the intellect.
sn4.19:3.10 | S: Where there is no mind, no ideas, no mind contact consciousness—you have no place there, Wicked One!” | T: Where no intellect exists, no ideas exist, no dimension of consciousness & contact at the intellect exists: There, Evil One, you cannot go.”
sn4.19:4.1 | S: “The things they call ‘mine’, | T: Māra: “Of what they say, ‘This is mine’;
sn4.19:4.2 | S: and those who say ‘it’s mine’: | T: and those who say, ‘Mine’:
sn4.19:4.3 | S: if your mind remains there, | T: If your intellect’s here,
sn4.19:4.4 | S: you won’t escape me, ascetic!” | T: contemplative, you can’t escape from me.”
sn4.19:5.1 | S: “The things they speak of aren’t mine; | T: The Buddha: “What they speak of isn’t mine,
sn4.19:5.2 | S: I’m not someone who speaks like that. | T: and I’m not one of those who speak it.
sn4.19:5.3 | S: So know this, Wicked One: | T: Know this, Evil One:
sn4.19:5.4 | S: you won’t even see the path I take.” | T: You won’t even see my tracks.”
sn4.19:6.1 | S: Then Māra … vanished right there. | T: Then Māra the Evil One—sad & dejected at realizing, “The Blessed One knows me; the One Well-Gone knows me”—vanished right there.

## sn4.1
sn4.1:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
sn4.1:1.2 | S: At one time, when he was first awakened, the Buddha was staying in Uruvelā at the root of the goatherd’s banyan tree on the bank of the Nerañjarā River. | T: on one occasion, when the Blessed One was newly self-awakened, he was dwelling near Uruvelā on the bank of the Nerañjarā River, at the root of the Goatherd's Banyan Tree.
sn4.1:1.3 | S: Then as he was in private retreat this thought came to his mind, | T: Then, when the Blessed One was alone in seclusion, this train of thought arose in his awareness,
sn4.1:1.4 | S: “I am truly freed from that grueling work! | T: “Released am I from that practice of extreme austerities!
sn4.1:1.5 | S: Thank goodness I’m freed from that pointless grueling work. | T: How good that I’m released from that useless practice of extreme austerities!
sn4.1:1.6 | S: Thank goodness I’m freed and have attained awakening.” | T: How good that I’m released, that I have attained awakening!”
sn4.1:2.1 | S: And then Māra the Wicked, knowing the Buddha’s train of thought, went up to him and addressed him in verse: | T: Then Māra the Evil One, having known with his awareness the train of thought in the Blessed One's awareness, went to the Blessed One and addressed him in verse:
sn4.1:3.1 | S: “You’ve left the practice of mortification | T: “Straying from the ascetic actions
sn4.1:3.2 | S: by which sons of men purify themselves. | T: by which young brahmans are purified,
sn4.1:3.3 | S: You’re impure, but think yourself pure; | T: impure, you suppose you’re pure.
sn4.1:3.4 | S: you’ve strayed from the path of purity.” | T: You’ve missed the path to purity.”
sn4.1:4.1 | S: Then the Buddha, knowing that this was Māra the Wicked, replied to him in verse: | T: Then, having understood that “This is Mara the Evil One,” the Blessed One replied to him in verses:
sn4.1:5.1 | S: “I realized that it’s pointless; | T: “Having known it to be useless,
sn4.1:5.2 | S: all that endless mortification | T: any further austerity,
sn4.1:5.3 | S: is as futile | T: each bringing nothing,
sn4.1:5.4 | S: as oars and rudder on dry land. | T: like oars & rudder on dry land:
sn4.1:6.1 | S: Ethics, immersion, and wisdom: | T: Developing virtue, concentration, & discernment,
sn4.1:6.2 | S: by developing this path to awakening | T: the path to awakening,
sn4.1:6.3 | S: I attained ultimate purity. | T: I’ve attained the foremost purity.
sn4.1:6.4 | S: You’re beaten, terminator!” | T: But you, End-maker, are struck down.”
sn4.1:7.1 | S: Then Māra the Wicked, thinking, “The Buddha knows me! The Holy One knows me!” miserable and sad, vanished right there. | T: Then Mara the Evil One—sad & dejected at realizing, “The Blessed One knows me, the One Well-Gone knows me”—vanished right there.

## sn4.20
sn4.20:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying in the land of the Kosalans, in a wilderness hut on the slopes of the Himalayas. | T: I have heard that on one occasion the Blessed One was staying among the Kosalans in a wilderness hut in a Himalayan district.
sn4.20:1.2 | S: Then as he was in private retreat this thought came to his mind, | T: Then, as he was alone in seclusion, this train of thought arose in his awareness:
sn4.20:1.3 | S: “I wonder if it’s possible to rule legitimately, without killing or having someone kill for you; without conquering or having someone conquer for you; without sorrowing or causing sorrow?” | T: “Is it possible to exercise rulership without killing or causing others to kill, without dispossessing or causing others to dispossess, without sorrowing or causing others sorrow—righteously?”
sn4.20:2.1 | S: And then Māra the Wicked, knowing the Buddha’s train of thought, went up to him and said, | T: Then Māra, the Evil One, knowing with his awareness the train of thought in the Blessed One’s awareness, went to him and on arrival said to him:
sn4.20:2.2 | S: “Rule, Blessed One! Rule, Holy One! Rule legitimately, without killing or having someone kill for you; without conquering or having someone conquer for you; without sorrowing or causing sorrow!” | T: “Exercise rulership, Blessed One! Exercise rulership, O One Well-Gone!—without killing or causing others to kill, without dispossessing or causing others to dispossess, without sorrowing or causing others sorrow—righteously!”
sn4.20:2.3 | S: “But what do you see, Wicked One, that you say this to me?” | T: “But what do you see in me, Evil One, that you say to me,
sn4.20:2.4 | P: ‘kāretu, bhante, bhagavā rajjaṁ, kāretu sugato, rajjaṁ ahanaṁ aghātayaṁ ajinaṁ ajāpayaṁ asocaṁ asocāpayaṁ dhammenā’”ti? | T: ‘Exercise rulership, Blessed One! Exercise rulership, O One Well-Gone!—without killing or causing others to kill, without dispossessing or causing others to dispossess, without sorrowing or causing others sorrow—righteously!’?”
sn4.20:2.5 | S: “The Blessed One, sir, has developed and cultivated the four bases for psychic power, made them a vehicle and a basis, kept them up, consolidated them, and properly implemented them. | T: “Lord, the Blessed One has developed the four bases of power, pursued them, given them a means of transport, given them a grounding, steadied them, consolidated them, and undertaken them well.
sn4.20:2.6 | S: If he wished, the Blessed One need only determine that the Himalaya, king of mountains, was gold, and it would turn into gold.” | T: If he wanted to, he could resolve on the Himalayas, king of mountains, as gold, and it would become a mountain of gold.”
sn4.20:3.1 | S: “Take a golden mountain, | T: The Buddha: “The entirety of a mountain of gold,
sn4.20:3.2 | S: entirely of native gold, and double it— | T: of solid bullion: Even twice that
sn4.20:3.3 | S: it’s still not enough for one! | T: wouldn’t suffice for one person.
sn4.20:3.4 | S: Knowing this, live a moral life. | T: Knowing this, live evenly, in tune with the contemplative life.
sn4.20:4.1 | S: When a personage has seen where suffering comes from | T: When you see stress, and from where it comes,
sn4.20:4.2 | S: how could they incline toward sensual pleasures? | T: how can you incline to sensuality?
sn4.20:4.3 | S: Realizing that attachment is a chain in the world, | T: Knowing acquisition to be a bond in the world,
sn4.20:4.4 | S: a personage would train to remove it.” | T: train for its subduing.”
sn4.20:5.1 | S: Then Māra the Wicked, thinking, “The Buddha knows me! The Holy One knows me!” miserable and sad, vanished right there. | T: Then Māra the Evil One—sad & dejected at realizing, “The Blessed One knows me; the One Well-Gone knows me”—vanished right there.
sn4.20:5.2 | P: Dutiyo vaggo. | T: (none)
sn4.20:6.0 | P: Tassuddānaṁ | T: (none)
sn4.20:6.1 | P: Pāsāṇo sīho sakalikaṁ, | T: (none)
sn4.20:6.2 | P: Patirūpañca mānasaṁ; | T: (none)
sn4.20:6.3 | P: Pattaṁ āyatanaṁ piṇḍaṁ, | T: (none)
sn4.20:6.4 | P: Kassakaṁ rajjena te dasāti. | T: (none)

## sn4.21
sn4.21:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
sn4.21:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying in the land of the Sakyans near Silāvatī. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying among the Sakyans at Silāvatī.
sn4.21:1.3 | S: Now at that time several mendicants were meditating not far from the Buddha, diligent, keen, and resolute. | T: And on that occasion a large number of monks were staying not far from the Blessed One: heedful, ardent, & resolute.
sn4.21:1.4 | S: Then Māra the Wicked manifested in the form of a brahmin with a large matted dreadlock, wearing an antelope hide. He was old, bent double, wheezing, and held a staff made of cluster fig tree wood. He went up to those mendicants and said, | T: Then Māra the Evil One, assuming the appearance of a brahman—with a large coiled top-knot, clad in an antelope hide, aged, crooked like a roof support, wheezing, holding a staff of fig wood—went to the monks and, on arrival, said to them,
sn4.21:1.5 | S: “You’ve gone forth while young, reverends.  With pristine black hair, you’re blessed with youth, in the prime of life, and you’ve never flirted with sensual pleasures. | T: “You have gone forth while young, masters—black-haired, endowed with the blessings of youth in the first stage of life—without having played with sensuality.
sn4.21:1.6 | S: Enjoy human sensual pleasures. | T: Enjoy human sensuality, monks.
sn4.21:1.7 | S: Don’t give up what is apparent in the present to chase after what takes effect over time.” | T: Don’t drop what is visible here-&-now in pursuit of what’s subject to time.”
sn4.21:1.8 | S: “Brahmin, that’s not what we’re doing. | T: “Brahman, we’re not dropping what’s visible here-&-now in pursuit of what’s subject to time.
sn4.21:1.9 | S: We’re giving up what takes effect over time to chase after what is apparent in the present. | T: We’re dropping what’s subject to time in pursuit of what’s visible here-&-now.
sn4.21:1.10 | S: For the Buddha says that sensual pleasures take effect over time; they give much suffering and distress, and they are all the more full of drawbacks. | T: For the Blessed One has said that sensuality is subject to time, of much stress, much despair, & greater drawbacks;
sn4.21:1.11 | S: But this teaching is apparent in the present life, immediately effective, inviting inspection, relevant, so that sensible people can know it for themselves.” | T: whereas this Dhamma is visible here-&-now, not subject to time, inviting all to come & see, pertinent, to be known by the observant for themselves.”
sn4.21:1.12 | S: When they had spoken, Māra the Wicked shook his head, waggled his tongue, raised his eyebrows until his brow puckered in three furrows, and left leaning on his staff. | T: When this was said, Māra the Evil One—shaking his head, wagging his tongue, raising his eyebrows so that his forehead was wrinkled in three furrows—left, leaning on his stick.
sn4.21:2.1 | S: Then those mendicants went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and told him what had happened. The Buddha said, | T: So the monks went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As they were sitting there they [told him what had happened].
sn4.21:2.2 | P: “idha mayaṁ, bhante, bhagavato avidūre appamattā ātāpino pahitattā viharāma. | T: (none)
sn4.21:2.3 | P: Atha kho, bhante, aññataro brāhmaṇo mahantena jaṭaṇḍuvena ajinakkhipanivattho jiṇṇo gopānasivaṅko ghurughurupassāsī udumbaradaṇḍaṁ gahetvā yena mayaṁ tenupasaṅkami; upasaṅkamitvā amhe etadavoca: | T: (none)
sn4.21:2.4 | P: ‘daharā bhavanto pabbajitā susū kāḷakesā bhadrena yobbanena samannāgatā paṭhamena vayasā anikkīḷitāvino kāmesu. | T: (none)
sn4.21:2.5 | P: Bhuñjantu bhavanto mānusake kāme. | T: (none)
sn4.21:2.6 | P: Mā sandiṭṭhikaṁ hitvā kālikaṁ anudhāvitthā’ti. | T: (none)
sn4.21:2.7 | P: Evaṁ vutte, mayaṁ, bhante, taṁ brāhmaṇaṁ etadavocumha: | T: (none)
sn4.21:2.8 | P: ‘na kho mayaṁ, brāhmaṇa, sandiṭṭhikaṁ hitvā kālikaṁ anudhāvāma. | T: (none)
sn4.21:2.9 | P: Kālikañca kho mayaṁ, brāhmaṇa, hitvā sandiṭṭhikaṁ anudhāvāma. | T: (none)
sn4.21:2.10 | P: Kālikā hi, brāhmaṇa, kāmā vuttā bhagavatā bahudukkhā bahupāyāsā, ādīnavo ettha bhiyyo. | T: (none)
sn4.21:2.11 | P: Sandiṭṭhiko ayaṁ dhammo akāliko ehipassiko opaneyyiko paccattaṁ veditabbo viññūhī’ti. | T: (none)
sn4.21:2.12 | P: Evaṁ vutte, bhante, so brāhmaṇo sīsaṁ okampetvā jivhaṁ nillāletvā tivisākhaṁ nalāṭe nalāṭikaṁ vuṭṭhāpetvā daṇḍamolubbha pakkanto”ti. | T: (none)
sn4.21:3.1 | S: “Mendicants, that was no brahmin. | T: “That wasn’t a brahman, monks.
sn4.21:3.2 | S: That was Māra the Wicked who came to pull the wool over your eyes!” | T: That was Māra the Evil One, come to blind you.”
sn4.21:3.3 | S: Then, understanding this matter, on that occasion the Buddha spoke this verse: | T: Then, on realizing the significance of that, the Blessed One on that occasion spoke these verses:
sn4.21:4.1 | S: “When a personage has seen where suffering comes from | T: One who has seen the cause from which suffering comes:
sn4.21:4.2 | S: how could they incline toward sensual pleasures? | T: How could that person incline to sensuality?
sn4.21:4.3 | S: Realizing that attachment is a chain in the world, | T: Having realized that acquisition is a tie in the world,
sn4.21:4.4 | S: a personage would train to remove it.” | T: a person should train to subdue just that.


